  "response": "## Yes — the finding is there\n\n`crime_00007` — **Missing Agent Context**, medium severity, confidence 0.90. It ranks 7th of 92 findings, and it's the only repo-level (rather than file-level) charge in the report.\n\n```\ntype:       missing_agent_context\ndetector:   missing_agent_context  (pack: universal)\nfile:       assets/cjs_shims.js        ← anchor file only, not where the problem lives\nagent_risk: 0.47   severity: 0.6   confidence: 0.9\n```\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n- no `AGENTS.md` at repo root\n- no `CLAUDE.md` at repo root\n- no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- no `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- `package.json` declares bins `tsup`, `tsup-node` — agents have no way to discover commands\n\nThe `assets/cjs_shims.js` path is just the anchor the repo-level detector attached itself to. Don't go looking in `assets/` — the gap is at the root.\n\n## Why this matters for your first PR\n\nThe finding is medium on its own. What makes it expensive is the rest of this scan, because the other 91 findings describe exactly the mistakes an uncontextualised agent will make here:\n\n**The test suite will lie to you.** 24 `weak_test_signal` findings — 12 tests with *zero* `expect`/`assert` calls (`test/example.test.ts:4`, `test/graphql.test.ts:4`, `test/shims.test.ts:5`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts:4`, and more), plus 12 that only use weak matchers. These are build-integration tests driven by `test/utils.ts`'s `run` helper; they invoke the bundler and mostly check it didn't throw. An agent that changes bundling behaviour, runs the suite, sees green, and reports \"tests pass\" is reporting nothing. Nothing in the repo says so.\n\n**Three files are load-bearing and unmarked.** `src/utils.ts` has fan-in 18 (`crime_00086`), `src/plugin.ts` fan-in 10 (`crime_00049`), `src/index.ts` fan-out 17 (`crime_00090`). `src/utils.ts` is also 424 lines. An agent doing a \"small cleanup\" in `utils.ts` touches 29 transitive importers with no test signal underneath it.\n\n**The entry points are enormous.** `build` in `src/index.ts:167` is 293 lines; `runEsbuild` in `src/esbuild/index.ts:61` is 237; `main` in `src/cli-main.ts:11` is 153. These are the exact bodies where \"an agent editing one section misses interactions in another.\" Which one is the public API vs. the CLI shim vs. the plugin host is discoverable only by reading all three.\n\n**The commands are undiscoverable.** The package ships two binaries. There is no stated build command, no test command, no note that `--dts` paths run a separate TypeScript/rollup pipeline (`src/api-extractor.ts`, `src/tsc.ts`, `src/rollup.ts`) from the esbuild path. An agent will guess, and a wrong guess here means either a no-op build or a 10-minute detour.\n\nNet effect: without an AGENTS.md, every agent that touches this repo re-derives the same map from scratch, gets the testing story wrong, and edits high-fan-in files with false confidence. That's the \"confident-but-wrong edits\" the detector is naming.\n\n## Minimal AGENTS.md that clears the finding\n\nRoot-level `AGENTS.md`. The detector checks for its presence at repo root, so this clears `crime_00007` outright — but write it to be actually true, not to satisfy a scanner.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\nTypeScript/JS bundler. Ships two binaries: `tsup`, `tsup-node`.\n\n## Commands\n\n- Install:   pnpm install\n- Build:     pnpm build\n- Test:      pnpm test          # integration: each test shells out a real build\n- Test one:  pnpm test <file>\n- Lint:      pnpm lint\n- Typecheck: pnpm typecheck\n\n## Layout\n\n- `src/index.ts`         — public API. `build()` is the orchestrator (~293 lines).\n- `src/cli-main.ts`      — CLI arg parsing → `build()`. No logic beyond parsing.\n- `src/esbuild/`         — the fast path. `runEsbuild()` + plugins (postcss, svelte, swc).\n- `src/rollup.ts`,\n  `src/tsc.ts`,\n  `src/api-extractor.ts` — the `--dts` path. Separate pipeline from esbuild.\n- `src/utils.ts`         — shared helpers. 18 importers; treat as frozen surface.\n- `src/plugin.ts`        — plugin host / lifecycle. 10 importers.\n- `test/utils.ts`        — `run()` builds a temp fixture project. All tests go through it.\n\n## Before you edit\n\n- `src/utils.ts`, `src/plugin.ts`, `src/log.ts` are high fan-in. Prefer additive\n  changes; read the importer list before restructuring.\n- `src/index.ts` `build()`, `src/esbuild/index.ts` `runEsbuild()`, and\n  `src/cli-main.ts` `main()` are long orchestrators. Changing one branch does not\n  mean the others are unaffected — read the whole body.\n- The esbuild path and the `--dts` path are independent. A fix in one is usually\n  not a fix in the other.\n\n## Testing\n\nTests are integration tests, not unit tests: they run a real build against a\ntemp fixture and mostly assert on emitted output. **A number of them assert\nnothing at all** — a green suite is not proof your change is correct. If you\nchange behaviour, add an assertion on the emitted artifact for the case you\ntouched.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- No new runtime dependencies without a reason in the PR description.\n- Errors: propagate or log with the error value attached. Several existing\n  `catch` blocks drop the error — don't copy that pattern.\n- Prefer async fs (`fs/promises`) in new code.\n\n## Don't\n\n- Don't reformat files you aren't otherwise changing.\n- Don't restructure `src/utils.ts` as a drive-by.\n```\n\nThat's roughly 50 lines and covers what the scan says is actually dangerous: undiscoverable commands, the two-pipeline split, the three high-fan-in files, and the hollow test suite.\n\nI haven't written this file — `04-monorepo` is an eval fixture, and adding an `AGENTS.md` to it would clear the finding the fixture exists to produce. Say the word if you want it dropped somewhere else.",
